The files themselves (native/core/fixtures/office-engine/{golden.json,MANIFEST.json}) are in the commit of this task.

## 4. Registering a library module (the four library tasks use this; nothing for the lead to apply now)

`src/functions/mod.rs` holds the list. A library task adds, in its own commit or through its fragment:

    mod math;                       // under "Library modules, one line each"   (pub mod if its twin test calls the functions directly)
    ...
    pub const TABLES: &[Table] = &[
        math::FUNCTIONS,            // one line per module
    ];

where its file declares `pub const FUNCTIONS: Table = &[("SUM", sum), ("NORM.DIST", norm_dist), ...];`: upper-case names, dotted
names included, `fn(&[Value]) -> Result<Value, Failure>`. A name registered in two modules is reported by the registry test
`the_library_lists_each_name_once_in_upper_case`. The shared argument conventions are `mixdog_xlsx_formula::coerce` (flatten,
parse_number, parse_bool, parse_date, parse_string, parse_number_array, parse_date_array, array_values_to_numbers, any_error,
any_is_error, clean_float, numbers, serial_to_date, date_to_serial, apply_criteria, meets_criteria, the JavaScript conversions
to_js_string / to_js_number / parse_float / js_loose_equals / js_strict_equals, create_matrix, fill_matrix, transpose, find_field,
get_matrix_size, initial, rest). Dates are `Value::Date(ms)` read in the local zone (`mixdog_js::time`).

## 5. The consumers (nr-office-xlsx-recalc and the writers that shift references)

    use mixdog_xlsx_formula::{Context, Extent, Failure, Value, evaluate_formula, formula_references,
        translate_shared_formula, is_blank, is_formula_error, UnsupportedFormula, MAX_RANGE_CELLS};

`evaluate_formula(formula, &mut impl Context) -> Result<Value, Failure>`: `Failure::Unsupported(UnsupportedFormula { reason })` is
Node's `UnsupportedFormula` (the reason text is Node's), `Failure::Thrown(message)` an exception Node let escape (a function that
throws, `A0:B1`); a caller that catches only refusals (xlsx-recalculate.mjs line 166) rethrows it. The result is a `Value`:
`Number`, `Text`, `Bool`, `Null` (a library answer of null), `Error(ErrorValue)` (an error value, message `ErrorValue::message()`).
`Context::sheet`, `value(sheet, "A1")`, `extent(sheet)` (Node `context.extent`), `defined_name(name)` (Node `context.definedName`,
None for a missing or empty definition). The dependency-chain depth and the circular-reference check stay in the caller, as in
xlsx-recalculate.mjs (`MAX_CHAIN_DEPTH`, `active`).

## 6. Test cases of xlsx-formula-engine.test.mjs that need library functions (add to tests/twin_engine.rs when the modules are registered)

These assertions use functions of the library tasks, so they could not run with the handful. Add them to a test next to
`the_engine_reads_ranges_calls_functions_and_keeps_if_from_inheriting_an_error`, evaluating with `mixdog_xlsx_formula::evaluate_formula`
(the library registry) over the same four cells (A1 2, A2 3, B1 "north", C1 TRUE), time zone Asia/Seoul:

    =ROUND(AVERAGE(A1:A2),1) -> 2.5
    =_xlfn.TEXTJOIN("-",TRUE,"a","b") -> "a-b"
    =YEAR(DATE(2024,5,6)) -> 2024
    =DAY(DATE(2024,5,6)) -> 6
    =ROUND(STDEV(A1:A2),4) -> 0.7071
    =RANK(A1,A1:A2) -> 2
    =_xlfn.RANK.EQ(A1,A1:A2) -> 2
    =COUNTIF(A1:A2,">2") -> 1
    =TEXT(1234.5,"0.00") -> "1234.50"

The workbook-level tests of that file (recalculateWithFormulaEngine: used range of a whole column, XML characters in a text answer,
another sheet, a column that waits on the row below, defined names from workbook.xml, array blocks, shared formulas, the report of
skipped cells, a circular reference) test portable/xlsx-recalculate.mjs, not this engine: they belong to the port of that file.

## 7. SERVICE_PENDING

No protocol method and no service: nothing to remove from SERVICE_PENDING.
